  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Late-inning rallies are starting to look like the only way this team wins.",
    "what_this_means": "The Giants led after three innings, ceded it in the fourth, then recaptured it for good in the eighth when Arraez sparked a four-run rally that broke the game open. Schmitt had already supplied the power. Two home runs, two RBI. But the night's real statement came from that eighth-inning sequence. This is a team that doesn't need to dominate early. It converts late chances when they come. Whisenhunt gave them five-plus clean innings. The bullpen. Brubaker, Miller, Kilian. Allowed nothing after he left. But the game belonged to the bats when it mattered most. They left runners stranded in the second, sixth, and seventh. None of it mattered because they scored when it counted. That distinction is becoming their identity: messy innings that don't cost them, one clear window when they finally deliver.",
    "what_to_watch": "They won by one run again. Three times in the last five games. That math gets harder each time.",
